<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Independent Commission of Experts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1996">1996</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Bergier Commission, Independent Commission of Experts Switzerland – Second World War</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Independent Commission of Experts, known as the Bergier Commission, was set up by the Swiss parliament in 1996 and chaired by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-francois-bergier">Jean-François Bergier</a> to investigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>'s role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Its reports, the last appearing in 2002, examined gold purchases from Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, dormant accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> victims and the turning back of refugees, and prompted a wide reassessment of the period.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The commission was formed on 12 December 1996, amid the World Jewish Congress's lawsuit against Swiss banks accused of withholding valuables belonging to victims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, in a decade of recurring criticism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>'s conduct towards Nazi Germany. Known also as the ICE or, from its German name, the UEK, it brought together Polish, American, Israeli and Swiss historians, among them Władysław Bartoszewski, Saul Friedländer, Harold James, Georg Kreis, Jacques Picard and Jakob Tanner. Parliament gave it unimpeded access to the archives of Swiss banks, insurers and other companies, forbade those firms to destroy files from the period, and raised its budget from 5 million to 22 million francs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Its final report, presented in March 2002, found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> had been the hub of the European gold trade, handling 77% of German gold shipments abroad, and that part of the gold the German state bank sold had been looted from private individuals and the central banks of occupied neighbours. Swiss banks lent to German firms engaged in armaments and in activities linked to the extermination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and some Swiss insurers dismissed Jewish employees in 1933 before German law required it. The commission judged that the figure of over 11,000 forced labourers and prisoners of war in Swiss subsidiaries was likely too low.</p><p>It concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> had contributed more to the German war effort than any other neutral country, having the largest presence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the lands it occupied, and that after 1945 banks and insurers had held to the letter of the law in refusing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a>, an attitude that allowed companies and individuals to profit from past injustice. It also found that Swiss diplomats applied a double standard, defending Swiss property strictly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> while accepting Germany's harsh treatment of Swiss Jews living there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The report was widely accepted as authoritative, but several conclusions were contested. Serge Klarsfeld argued in 2013 that Swiss authorities turned away closer to 3,000 Jewish refugees, against the 24,500 of an earlier report and the 20,000 of the final one. The final report kept the J-stamp in Jewish passports but no longer called it a Swiss invention. Jean-Christian Lambelet questioned its consistency and statistics, and Philippe Marguerat argued that it overlooked the gold purchases' value as a deterrent to invasion. The Working Group of Lived History, some 500 Swiss, many of whom had lived through the war, answered it with oral testimony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bergier commission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Old debates were reopened in the 1990s, when the <b>Independent Commission of Experts</b> chaired by Jean-François Bergier examined Swiss gold dealings with Nazi Germany and the restrictive treatment of refugees; its final report appeared in 2002, and the major banks agreed in 1998 to a settlement over dormant…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
